<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Lubavians</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1260 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Prussian territory around Lubawa on the border with Masovia, given to the first missionary bishop of Prussia in 1216.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubawa">Lubawa</a> lay in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, next to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno land</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>. Its people were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a>, related to the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pomesanians">Pomesanians</a> and Sasins, and lived in scattered settlements around strongholds.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded of them. Exposed to Polish influence, some of their leaders accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>: in 1216 Prussian nobles granted the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubawa">Lubawa</a> to Christian, the Cistercian missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, making it one of the first mission territories.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The area passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> during its conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in the 1230s and was assigned to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bishopric-of-culm">Bishopric of Culm</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> resided at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubawa">Löbau</a> (Lubawa). The Prussian population merged with German and Polish settlers.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 800 CE – 1260 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:75.62%;width:9.15%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1260 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baltic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Prussian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lubawa, Lubava land</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1030&amp;focus=lubavians" aria-label="Lubavians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5125 389 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lubavians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5451 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5445 645h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5445 645h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Poland. <a href="/atlas?year=1030&amp;focus=lubavians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

